  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nA cold front continues to drift southeastward across southern South \nDakota early this afternoon. Shower and thunderstorm activity \ncontinued to accompany this front through midday with most of it now \nsouth of I-90 and exiting out far southern areas. In the front's \nwake, northwest winds and turned gusty this afternoon. Some drier \nair has tried to advance east out of central SD but dew point \ntemperatures across northeast SD and west central MN remain in the \nlow mid 60s F. Locales where elevated fire weather concerns lie this \nafternoon have seen northwest wind gusts between 20-30 mph and RH \nvalues fall into the upper teens to low 20s percent range. These \nconditions are likely to persist into this evening maintaining the \nfire danger threat before temperatures cool and winds gradually \ndiminish. The Red Flag Warning for north central SD will remain \nposted.\n \nHeading into the overnight and Tuesday, sfc high pressure will \ngradually nose in from the northwest through the next 24 hours. It \nwon't build in quick enough tonight to shut off the winds \ncompletely. An upper trough is progged to brush across are far \nnortheast overnight and moreso across eastern ND and northern MN. \nThere will be some shower activity associated with this feature that \nshould mainly stay confined out of our forecast area so have went \nwith a dry forecast for now. But, something for the evening shift to \nmonitor. A cooler and drier air mass will take hold for Tuesday into \nWednesday as the high builds southeast and centers itself across the \nEastern Dakotas and western MN. Some s/w energy will ride the \nnorthwest flow and over the western CONUS ridge into western SD late \nTuesday into Wednesday. This could provide a chance for some shower \nand thunderstorms to portions of our south central SD zones. There \nis a 20-40 percent chance for seeing at least a tenth of an inch of \nmoisture but it remains confined from an Eagle Butte to Pierre to \nGann Valley line and points south and west. Outside of those \nlocales, a dry forecast looks likely to continue. Some more areas \ncould stand a better shot at moisture on Thursday into early Friday \nas another mid level disturbance rolls southeast across the western \nand central portions of SD.\n\nBy late week and through this weekend, upper ridging is progged to \nbuild back overhead across the Northern Plains. After near normal \ntemperatures through about Thursday, another wave of above normal \nheat is projected to return to the area. Some model thermal progs \nindicate 850mb temperatures will climb into the upper 20s C to lower \n30s C. This should lead to widespread temperatures in the 90s to low \n100s, with the hottest readings across central SD. Unfortunately, \nlittle to no chances for moisture are expected late in the week and \nwith this potential heat on the way again, that doesn't bode well \nfor those areas dealing with ongoing drought conditions.",
